development.question_text:
A circle, centre $O$, has diameter $16\text{ cm}$.
$T$ is the point of contact of tangent $TP$, and $OP = 17\text{ cm}$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.38]
\usetikzlibrary{calc}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (T) at (0,8);
\coordinate (P) at (15,8);
\draw (O) circle (8);
\fill (O) circle (1.5pt);
\node[below left] at (O) {$O$};
\draw (O) -- (T);
\draw (T) -- (P) node[midway, above] {$x$};
\draw (O) -- (P) node[midway, below right] {$17\text{ cm}$};
\node[above left] at (T) {$T$};
\node[right] at (P) {$P$};
\coordinate (ta) at ($(T)!0.3cm!(O)$);
\coordinate (tp) at ($(T)!0.3cm!(P)$);
\draw (ta) -- ($(ta)+(tp)-(T)$) -- (tp);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Find the length of $x = TP$.

mastery.question_text:
$PT$ and $PQ$ are tangents to the circle, centre $O$, from the external point $P$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.42]
\usetikzlibrary{calc, decorations.markings}
\tikzset{tickmark/.style={postaction={decorate, decoration={markings, mark=at position 0.5 with {\draw[black] (0pt,-4pt) -- (0pt,4pt);}}}}}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (P) at (13,0);
\coordinate (T1) at (1.923,4.615);
\coordinate (T2) at (1.923,-4.615);
\draw (O) circle (5);
\fill (O) circle (1.5pt);
\node[left] at (O) {$O$};
\draw (O) -- (T1);
\draw (O) -- (T2);
\draw (P) -- (T1);
\draw (P) -- (T2);
\node[above] at (T1) {$T$};
\node[below] at (T2) {$Q$};
\node[right] at (P) {$P$};
\coordinate (t1o) at ($(T1)!0.25cm!(O)$);
\coordinate (t1p) at ($(T1)!0.25cm!(P)$);
\draw (t1o) -- ($(t1o)+(t1p)-(T1)$) -- (t1p);
\coordinate (t2o) at ($(T2)!0.25cm!(O)$);
\coordinate (t2p) at ($(T2)!0.25cm!(P)$);
\draw (t2o) -- ($(t2o)+(t2p)-(T2)$) -- (t2p);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Prove that $\triangle PTO \equiv \triangle PQO$, and hence that $PT = PQ$.

$PT$ and $PQ$ are tangents to the circle, centre $O$, with $OT = OQ = 9\text{ cm}$ and $PT = PQ = 12\text{ cm}$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.35]
\usetikzlibrary{calc, decorations.markings}
\tikzset{tickmark/.style={postaction={decorate, decoration={markings, mark=at position 0.5 with {\draw[black] (0pt,-4pt) -- (0pt,4pt);}}}}}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (P) at (15,0);
\coordinate (T1) at (5.4,7.2);
\coordinate (T2) at (5.4,-7.2);
\draw (O) circle (9);
\fill (O) circle (1.5pt);
\node[left] at (O) {$O$};
\draw[tickmark] (O) -- (T1) node[midway, above left] {$9\text{ cm}$};
\draw[tickmark] (O) -- (T2) node[midway, below left] {$9\text{ cm}$};
\draw (P) -- (T1) node[midway, above right] {$12\text{ cm}$};
\draw (P) -- (T2) node[midway, below right] {$12\text{ cm}$};
\node[above] at (T1) {$T$};
\node[below] at (T2) {$Q$};
\node[right] at (P) {$P$};
\coordinate (t1o) at ($(T1)!0.25cm!(O)$);
\coordinate (t1p) at ($(T1)!0.25cm!(P)$);
\draw (t1o) -- ($(t1o)+(t1p)-(T1)$) -- (t1p);
\coordinate (t2o) at ($(T2)!0.25cm!(O)$);
\coordinate (t2p) at ($(T2)!0.25cm!(P)$);
\draw (t2o) -- ($(t2o)+(t2p)-(T2)$) -- (t2p);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
(a) Show that $OP = 15\text{ cm}$.
(b) In a different diagram, $PT$ and $PQ$ are tangents to a circle, centre $O$, with $\angle TOQ = 100^{\circ}$.
Find $\angle TPQ$.

The incircle of $\triangle PQR$, centre $I$, touches side $PQ$ at $X$, side $QR$ at $Y$, and side $RP$ at $Z$.
$PQ = 13\text{ cm}$, $QR = 14\text{ cm}$ and $RP = 15\text{ cm}$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{calc, decorations.markings}
\tikzset{tickmark/.style={postaction={decorate, decoration={markings, mark=at position 0.5 with {\draw[black] (0pt,-4pt) -- (0pt,4pt);}}}}, tickmark2/.style={postaction={decorate, decoration={markings, mark=at position 0.5 with {\draw[black] (-2pt,-4pt) -- (-2pt,4pt); \draw[black] (2pt,-4pt) -- (2pt,4pt);}}}}, tickmark3/.style={postaction={decorate, decoration={markings, mark=at position 0.5 with {\draw[black] (-3pt,-4pt) -- (-3pt,4pt); \draw[black] (0pt,-4pt) -- (0pt,4pt); \draw[black] (3pt,-4pt) -- (3pt,4pt);}}}}}
\coordinate (Q) at (0,0);
\coordinate (R) at (5.6,0);
\coordinate (P) at (2,4.8);
\coordinate (I) at (2.4,1.6);
\coordinate (X) at (0.923,2.215);
\coordinate (Y) at (2.4,0);
\coordinate (Z) at (3.68,2.56);
\draw (I) circle (1.6);
\fill (I) circle (1.5pt);
\node[right] at (I) {$I$};
\draw[tickmark] (Q) -- (X);
\draw[tickmark] (Q) -- (Y);
\draw[tickmark2] (P) -- (X);
\draw[tickmark2] (P) -- (Z);
\draw[tickmark3] (R) -- (Y);
\draw[tickmark3] (R) -- (Z);
\draw (Q) -- (P) -- (R) -- cycle;
\node[below left] at (Q) {$Q$};
\node[below right] at (R) {$R$};
\node[above] at (P) {$P$};
\node[left] at (X) {$X$};
\node[below] at (Y) {$Y$};
\node[right] at (Z) {$Z$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Find the length of $QY$.
Hint: set up a system of three simultaneous equations.
